{
 "_about": "Gladstone-Dale constants k (specific refractive energy per wt% of constituent), from Mandarino (1981) Can. Mineral. 19, 441-450, TABLE 7 'Revised Gladstone-Dale constants' (p. 446). That paper states its Table 7 'should replace all former constants', and this file now follows it throughout. TRANSCRIBED FROM THE SCAN and checked three ways: every row's printed molecular weight against the one computed from its formula (all 114 agree; Pm2O3 342 vs 338 is the paper's own figure, Pm having no stable isotope), sixteen values against the paper's abstract, and the whole file against the corpus — 146 of 255 papers that print n, a density and an analysis now reproduce their OWN published compatibility index, against 110 before. 'reliability' is Mandarino's indicator: H high, M medium, L low, ? derived by extrapolation or interpolation ('extrapolated'). 'variants' are Table 7's own alternative values for a named class of minerals (it prints one general k and, for some constituents, a second for sulfates & selenates, silicates, or neso- and inosilicates) plus, where they differ, the value the owner's own spreadsheets used; nothing chooses between them automatically, so a reviewer picks one with --k. TWO OWNER VALUES WERE SUPERSEDED, both on measurement rather than deference. UO3 0.134 -> 0.118 (Table 7, reliability H): of the 22 corpus papers whose analysis carries UO3, 12 reproduce their own published compatibility index on 0.118 against 5 on 0.134, and the matches are near-exact (stated -0.005 vs -0.006, +0.008 vs +0.011). MgO 0.225 -> 0.200 gains one paper. The 0.134 the spreadsheets use for uranyl minerals is not in Table 7; it is kept as a variant, '--k UO3=0.134' reaches it, and tests.test_gd pins that the owner's spanoite sheet still comes out superior that way. Worth the owner's eye: this is a working convention overturned on corpus evidence. Ammonium is keyed N2H8O, which is what epma.parse_constituent normalises (NH4)2O to; '(NH4)2O' is an alias. Table 7's O2- (0.203) is deliberately left out: the oxides already carry their oxygen, so a constant for bare O would double-count an analysis tabulated in elements.",
